cite&sdt=a&sl=0&origin=inward&editSaveSearch=&txGid=81568e733d0770b727ec9511568d800d#1"},{"title":"Tuning of Band Gap of Cathode Li2NiPO4F by Replacing P to Nb and Forming Li2NiNbO4F for Application as 5 V Cathode in Lithium Ion Battery: A Density Functional Theory Study-Reference-Cited by-同舟云学术 - Tuning of Band Gap of Cathode Li2NiPO4F by Replacing P to Nb and Forming Li2NiNbO4F for Application as 5 V Cathode in Lithium Io...","url":"http://www.globalauthorid.com/WebPortal/ArticleView?wd=D4F1376713404D1226D15329E4D5041D80F2A6C485D9FF39826665044E49526F#1"},{"title":"Lithium transition metal fluorophosphates (Li2CoPO4F and Li2NiPO4F) as cathode materials for lithium ion battery from atomistic simulation - About this region","url":"https://journals.scholarsportal.info/details/00224596/v204icomplete/329_ltmfallibfas.xml&sub=all#1"}]  ## Summary  **Li₂NiPO₄F as a high-voltage cathode.** Based on the search results, Li₂NiPO₄F is a lithium nickel fluorophosphate studied as an ultra-high-voltage cathode for Li-ion batteries. A 2024 DFT study (Chakrabarti & Thakur, *J. Electrochem. Soc.* 171, 080508) calculated its electrochemical properties as:  - **Voltage: 5.33 V** (matching the "5.3 V" in the query) - Electronic band gap: 4.0 eV - Capacity: ~287.3 mAh g⁻¹ (for extraction of 2 Li⁺) - Energy density: 1531.31 Wh kg⁻¹  The same study notes that the large band gap (4.0 eV) limits commercial application, and proposes replacing P with Nb (forming Li₂NiNbO₄F) to lower the band gap (3.64 eV) at a reduced voltage of 5.01 V.  **Fedotov and Khasanova's contributions.** The results show Khasanova and Fedotov as authors on related fluorophosphate work rather than on the 5.33 V DFT paper specifically:  - **Fedotov et al. (2017, *J. Phys. Chem. C* 121, 3194–3202)** studied the isostructural **Li₂CoPO₄F** high-voltage cathode (space group *Pnma*; a = 10.4528 Å, b = 6.3867 Å, c = 10.8764 Å) using neutron powder diffraction, Voronoi–Dirichlet partitioning, bond valence sums, and DFT, finding low Li-hopping barriers (0.12–0.43 eV) and a possible 3D Li-ion migration channel. The paper used the **isostructural Li₂NiPO₄F single-crystal structure as the starting model** for the Li₂CoPO₄F refinement. - **Antipov, Khasanova, Fedotov (2015, *IUCrJ*)** reviewed Li and transition-metal fluoride phosphates, noting their high redox potentials (~5 V) and listing, e.g., Li₂CoPO₄F at 5.1 V (143 mAh g⁻¹). The review highlights that practical use is hampered by the lack of stable high-voltage electrolytes, electrolyte side-reactions (low Coulombic efficiency for Co-based materials), and more complex/costly synthesis. - **Khasanova et al. (2011)** is cited in the literature (e.g., in the atomistic simulation paper and as the source of the Li₂NiPO₄F structure) for characterization of Li₂NiPO₄F. - **Khasanova, Drozhzhin, Fedotov et al. (2013, *Beilstein J. Nanotechnol.* 4, 860–867)** reported synthesis and electrochemical performance of Li₂Co₁₋ₓMₓPO₄F (M = Fe, Mn) cathode materials.  **Supporting computational work.** Lee & Park (2013, *J. Solid State Chem.* 204, 329–334) performed atomistic simulations on Li₂MPO₄F (M = Co, Ni, both *Pnma*), reproducing experimental unit cells, predicting a 3D Li⁺ diffusion network, and substantial Li/Co or Li/Ni antisite defect formation. They note that the higher ionicity of metal–F bonds raises the operating potential, making these materials active above 5 V via the second Li⁺ cycling step (Li₁M PO₄F ↔ M PO₄F).  In short, the "5.3 V" figure for Li₂NiPO₄F comes from DFT calculations, and the Fedotov/Khasanova group's experimental and review work places this nickel fluorophosphate within the broader family of high-voltage (>5 V) Li₂MPO₄F cathode materials (mostly studied in the Co analogue), whose practical realization depends on developing stable high-voltage electrolytes.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
