te Fluorides.","url":"https://openurl.ebsco.com/EPDB%3Agcd%3A5%3A40112501/detailv2?sid=ebsco%3Aocu%3Arecord&id=ebsco%3Adoi%3A10.1002%2Fchem.201900186&bquery=AU%20Erhard%2C%20A.&page=1&link_origin=none&searchDescription=Erhard%2C%20A.&crl=f"},{"title":"Search Results (2,239) - (This article belongs to the Special Issue Advanced Materials in Drug Release and Drug Delivery Systems)","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/search?sort=pubdate&page_no=41&page_count=50&year_from=1996&year_to=2026&q=metal%20profile&view=default#2"},{"title":"OUCI - 15 publications · Export","url":"https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/?backlinks_to=10.1016/j.electacta.2012.12.095#1"},{"title":"Search Results (516) - Structure, Shift in Redox Potential and Li-Ion Diffusion Behavior in Tavorite LiFe1−xVxPO4F Solid-Solution Cathodes","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/search?sort=pubdate&page_no=10&page_count=50&year_from=1996&year_to=2025&q=hydro%E2%80%93thermal%20coupling&view=default#2"},{"title":"Search Results (38,122) - Structure, Shift in Redox Potential and Li-Ion Diffusion Behavior in Tavorite LiFe1−xVxPO4F Solid-Solution Cathodes","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/search?sort=pubdate&page_no=652&page_count=50&year_from=1996&year_to=2024&q=dominant&view=default#4"}]  ## Summary of Search Results: Tavorite LiNiPO₄F  **Important caveat:** The provided search results contain **no direct report of a tavorite-structured LiNiPO₄F**. The only nickel-containing fluorophosphate discussed is **Li₂NiPO₄F**, which is described as *not* tavorite-type. The results do, however, provide substantial context on related tavorite fluorophosphates/fluorosulfates, their synthesis, structures, and voltages.  ### Li₂NiPO₄F (the closest nickel compound in the results) - Per the *Lithium Batteries: Science and Technology* source, Li₂NiPO₄F is **isostructural with Li₂CoPO₄F**, crystallizing in the **orthorhombic system (space group Pnma, Z = 8)** — not the triclinic tavorite framework. Li₂CoPO₄F features **CoO₄F₂ (i.e., MO₄F₂) octahedra** rather than MO₆ octahedra, and two distinct Li sites (4c and 8d). - **Voltage/electrochemistry:** Li₂CoPO₄F and Li₂NiPO₄F are described as a new class of **~5 V cathode materials**. The theoretical intercalation voltage for Li₂CoPO₄F is **~4.9 V** (observed plateau at ~5 V), and **experimentally, LiNiPO₄F discharge voltage is demonstrated to be close to 5.3 V**. A theoretical capacity upper limit of **~310 mAh g⁻¹** is expected for both Li₂CoPO₄F and Li₂NiPO₄F. - **Synthesis difficulty:** Synthesis of Li₂MPO₄F remains difficult, requiring either **ion exchange of Na-counterparts** or a **lengthy solid-state reaction (at least 10 h heat treatment)**. For Li₂CoPO₄F, spark plasma sintering shortened the reaction to 9 min (yielding submicrometric 0.8 μm particles); a two-step solid-state method followed by ZrO₂ (5 wt%) coating gave up to 144 mAh g⁻¹ (2–5.2 V at 10 mA g⁻¹). A high irreversible capacity from electrolyte decomposition at high anodic potentials is noted as a drawback for these high-voltage fluorophosphates.  ### Tavorite structure and related fluorophosphates/fluorosulfates - **Tavorite concept:** The results emphasize that tavorite-structured lithium-metal fluorophosphates are promising alternatives to olivine cathodes, showing **exceptional ionic conductivity, excellent thermal stability, and capacity retention**. Rietveld refinement on tavorite LiFePO₄F indicates **open pathways for 3D ion transport** in both the charged and discharged (Li₂FePO₄F) states. Tavorite phases discussed are **triclinic** (e.g., triclinic LiFe₁₋ₓVₓPO₄F). - **Iron tavorite (LiFePO₄F):** Two-step solid-state synthesis gives high-purity tavorite LiFePO₄F; galvanostatic cycling shows **voltage plateaus at 2.71/2.86 V** (Fe³⁺/²⁺ redox, two-phase transition) with low polarization (0.15 V). The Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺ couple in iron tavorite lies between **2.5–2.9 V vs Li⁺/Li** (theoretical capacity 150 mAh g⁻¹), and is strongly affected by the amount of fluoride coordinated to iron; LiFePO₄F₁₋ₓ(OH)ₓ solid solutions show the best high-C-rate behavior. A **fluorolytic sol–gel route** (nonaqueous, low-temperature) also produces tavorite LiFePO₄F with excellent cycling stability (115 mAh g⁻¹ after 100 cycles at C/2), and the same route yielded Li₂CoPO₄F. - **Vanadium tavorite (LiVPO₄F):** Described as a higher-voltage tavorite cathode (~4 V class per the AVPO₄F paper), with an **8.5% unit-cell volume change between charged and discharged forms**, a specific energy of **655 Wh/kg**, and a volumetric energy density of ~2140 Wh/L; it also shows reversible capacity near 2 V (multiredox behavior). LiFe₁₋ₓVₓPO₄F solid solutions (x = 0–1, one-step solid-state synthesis) exhibit single-phase behavior for 0 < x < 1, with redox potential governed by M electronegativity (Fe²⁺/³⁺) or M–O₄F₂ bond length (V³⁺/⁴⁺). - **Other synthesis routes mentioned:** Ion exchange using LiBr in ethanol to make Li₂₋ₓNaₓFe[PO₄]F tavorite; supercritical methanol (300 °C, 15 min) topotactic insertion of LiF into szomolnokite-type FeSO₄·H₂O for tavorite LiFeSO₄F (with phase transformation to triplite at longer times/higher temperatures); microwave heating producing nanocrystalline tavorite LiFeSO₄F; hydrothermal synthesis of tavorite LiVPO₄OH; and eutectic salt melts for LiFePO₄F.  ### Bottom line Based on these results: **no tavorite-structured LiNiPO₄F synthesis or electrochemistry is documented**. Nickel fluorophosphate appears only as **Li₂NiPO₄F (orthorhombic, Pnma; ~5.3 V discharge vs Li; ~310 mAh g⁻¹ theoretical capacity; difficult to synthesize, requiring ion exchange or ≥10 h solid-state routes)**. Tavorite fluorophosphate chemistry in the results centers on Fe and V analogs (LiFePO₄F, LiVPO₄F, LiFe₁₋ₓVₓPO₄F, LiFePO₄OH-type phases), which are triclinic, offer 3D Li-ion transport and high ionic conductivity, and span redox voltages from ~2.5–2.9 V (Fe) up to the ~4 V region (V).   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
