r lithium ion battery from atomistic simulation - Skip to main contentSkip to article","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022459613002922#1"},{"title":"Alkali/Transition Metal Halo-And Hydroxy-Phosphates And Related Electrode Active Materials - Eureka | Patsnap - Alkali/Transition Metal Halo-And Hydroxy-Phosphates And Related Electrode Active Materials","url":"https://eureka.patsnap.com/patent-US20070190425A1#1"},{"title":"Lithium transition metal fluorophosphates (Li2CoPO4F and Li2NiPO4F) as cathode materials for lithium ion battery from atomistic simulation","url":"https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/edb906cb-4388-3921-94e8-2d80a9abe50b/"}]  ## Li₂NiPO₄F as a 5.3 V Cathode: Summary of Search Results  **Experimental confirmation of the ~5.3 V redox potential.** Nagahama, Hasegawa, and Okada (*J. Electrochem. Soc.* 157, A748, 2010, DOI: 10.1149/1.3417068) reported "High Voltage Performances of Li₂NiPO₄F Cathode with Dinitrile-Based Electrolytes." Because standard carbonate electrolytes oxidize below the operating potential of this material, they used a sebaconitrile-based electrolyte (1 M LiBF₄ in ethylene carbonate–dimethyl carbonate–sebaconitrile, 25:25:50 by vol%), which showed excellent electrochemical stability above 6 V vs Li/Li⁺. As a validation, LiFePO₄ was intentionally charged to 6 V and still delivered 137 mAh/g with 91% coulombic efficiency. Using this oxidation-resistant electrolyte, they demonstrated via cyclic voltammetry and charge/discharge cycling that Li₂NiPO₄F indeed has a redox potential of approximately **5.3 V vs Li/Li⁺**. Prior to this work, Li₂NiPO₄F cathode properties had not been reported, presumably because of the electrolyte stability problem.  **Theoretical support for the high voltage.** A 2024 density functional theory (DFT) study by Chakrabarti and Thakur (*J. Electrochem. Soc.* 171, 080508, DOI: 10.1149/1945-7111/ad69c8) calculated Li₂NiPO₄F properties: voltage **5.33 V**, electronic band gap 4.0 eV, capacity ~287.3 mAh/g (for 2 Li⁺ extraction), and energy density 1531.31 Wh/kg. The authors note, however, that the large electronic band gap would limit commercial application. Substituting P with Nb (forming Li₂NiNbO₄F) reduces the band gap to 3.64 eV but also lowers the voltage to 5.01 V, with 215.71 mAh/g and 1080.71 Wh/kg.  **Structural/transport insights.** An atomistic simulation study by Lee and Park (*J. Solid State Chem.* 204, 329–334, 2013) on Li₂MPO₄F (M = Co, Ni) found that these fluorophosphates have a **3D Li⁺ ion diffusion network**, forecasting good Li⁺ conductivity, and that Li/Co or Li/Ni antisite defects form readily at substantial concentrations. The Introduction notes that high-voltage (>5 V) activity in the Pnma-structured Li₂CoPO₄F and Li₂NiPO₄F arises from cycling of the second Li⁺ ion (Li₁M PO₄F ↔ MPO₄F), enabled by the increased ionicity of metal–F bonds.  **Broader context.** A review of Li and transition-metal fluoride phosphates (PMC4285883) notes that investigation of these materials is hampered by their high redox potentials (~5 V), and a patent (US20070190425A1) describes a synthesis route to Li₂NiPO₄F via a LiNiPO₄ precursor reacted with LiF.  **Bottom line:** Li₂NiPO₄F is a high-voltage fluorophosphate cathode operating near **5.3 V vs Li/Li⁺** (experimentally confirmed by Nagahama et al. using a >6 V-stable dinitrile electrolyte; theoretically 5.33 V by DFT), with a large theoretical capacity from two Li⁺ per formula unit, but practical use is constrained by electrolyte oxidation at such high potentials and by its large electronic band gap.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
