~4.17 V.  **Experimental Mg-doping results:** - A fast carbothermal reduction reaction has been used to synthesize Mg²⁺-doped LiVPO₄F@C cathodes with superior electrochemical performance. Mg substitution of V raises the pyrolytic temperature by ~40°C and improves Li⁺ diffusivity by two times at the discharge plateau. The LiV₀.₉₇Mg₀.₀₃PO₄F@C material delivers a high discharge capacity of 140.3 mAh g⁻¹ at 1 C, a high discharge potential of 4.2 V, and 88.2% capacity retention after 500 cycles. - A "double-donor" doping strategy (carbon-coated LiVPO₄F with Mg²⁺ substituting Li⁺ and/or Nb⁵⁺ substituting V³⁺) transforms the material from p-type to n-type, as confirmed by the Hall effect. The Li₀.₉₉₅Mg₀.₀₀₅V₀.₉₈Nb₀.₀₂PO₄F cathode shows greatly enhanced electronic and ionic conductivity, improved Li⁺ diffusion, and reduced polarization, achieving an initial discharge capacity of 124.5 mAh g⁻¹ with 97.3% retention after 600 cycles at 1.6C; at 8C, it delivered 392 Wh kg⁻¹ initially with 84.4% retention after 2000 cycles.  **Theoretical (first-principles) insights:** DFT calculations have systematically investigated Na, Mg, and Al doping in LiVPO₄F, using models including Li₀.₇₅Mg₀.₁₂₅VPO₄F (Mg replacing two Li atoms, with one Li removed to create a vacancy for charge neutrality) and α-, β-, and γ-LiMg₀.₃₇₅V₀.₇₅PO₄F (Mg substituting V 1a/1b sites with interstitial compensation). These studies examined effects on structure, electronic properties, defect properties, and Li-ion migration.  Overall, the results indicate that Mg doping (alone or combined with carbon coating or co-doping) enhances the thermodynamic stability, conductivity, Li-ion diffusivity, and cycling performance of tavorite LiVPO₄F cathodes.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.
